Player one's gonna try to go down before player two.

263
00:10:30,540 --> 00:10:32,740
Player one may immediately try to go down

264
00:10:32,740 --> 00:10:34,780
and then say, sorry, I don't change my mind.

265
00:10:34,780 --> 00:10:35,940
Once I start, I start.

266
00:10:35,940 --> 00:10:37,700
I don't ever back down, right?

267
00:10:37,700 --> 00:10:39,700
And then player two may say, well, what a coincidence.

268
00:10:39,700 --> 00:10:42,260
I'm the same way, except I'm playing right.

269
00:10:42,260 --> 00:10:46,380
And then you may have a standoff, right?

270
00:10:46,380 --> 00:10:50,500
So all the Nash says is that these are the two pure strategy

271
00:10:50,500 --> 00:10:52,580
Nash equilibrium.

272
00:10:52,580 --> 00:10:53,340
OK.

273
00:10:53,340 --> 00:10:53,820
All right.

274
00:10:53,820 --> 00:10:55,580
And again, how is this different from strict dominance?

275
00:10:55,580 --> 00:10:57,900
Because there isn't a strictly dominant thing.

276
00:10:57,900 --> 00:11:01,820
So again, just to make sure I'm following entirely

277
00:11:01,820 --> 00:11:02,540
and everything.

278
00:11:02,540 --> 00:11:04,900
So player one, it's always played sequentially

279
00:11:04,900 --> 00:11:06,580
and player one always goes first.

280
00:11:06,580 --> 00:11:07,100
So no.

281
00:11:07,100 --> 00:11:10,020
So actually, so here, actually what you'll see

282
00:11:10,020 --> 00:11:11,220
is that there is no sequence.

283
00:11:11,220 --> 00:11:13,700
Sequence is not even shown here.

284
00:11:13,700 --> 00:11:15,500
Sequence is not even shown in this diagram.

285
00:11:15,500 --> 00:11:17,540
So hypothetically, player two could go first.

286
00:11:17,540 --> 00:11:18,060
Yes, yes.

287
00:11:18,060 --> 00:11:22,140
So if this were a game where player two got to go first,

288
00:11:22,140 --> 00:11:25,380
then we would be able to solve this by backwards deduction.

289
00:11:25,380 --> 00:11:26,220
So think about this.

290
00:11:26,220 --> 00:11:29,260
Yes, so if there were an order, say,

291
00:11:29,260 --> 00:11:36,540
we're first up, down, and then we're left, right, left, right,

292
00:11:36,540 --> 00:11:38,980
then actually we could solve it by strict dominance

293
00:11:38,980 --> 00:11:41,140
and we'd have no either Nash.

294
00:11:41,140 --> 00:11:44,580
And then it would just be, we can just work this out.

295
00:11:44,580 --> 00:11:47,740
Yes, so up left, so 10, 10.

296
00:11:47,740 --> 00:11:53,020
And then, let's see, up right, 0, 15.

297
00:11:53,020 --> 00:11:56,860
And then down left, 15, 0.

298
00:11:56,860 --> 00:11:59,660
And then finally, negative 5, negative 5.

299
00:11:59,660 --> 00:12:01,580
And then, yes, this is actually a good chance

300
00:12:01,580 --> 00:12:03,580
to solve another thing by backwards deduction.

301
00:12:03,580 --> 00:12:06,020
So let's just do it together.

302
00:12:06,020 --> 00:12:08,380
So over here, so at this node, which thing

303
00:12:08,380 --> 00:12:09,420
is never going to happen?

304
00:12:09,420 --> 00:12:11,700
Remember, this is player one.

305
00:12:11,700 --> 00:12:14,020
This is player two.

306
00:12:14,020 --> 00:12:16,140
So player two gets to here.

307
00:12:16,140 --> 00:12:17,740
Which thing is he never going to do?

308
00:12:17,740 --> 00:12:18,240
L.

309
00:12:18,240 --> 00:12:21,140
Yeah, he's never going to do L, so we can erase that thing.

310
00:12:21,140 --> 00:12:23,940
And over here, which one will player two never do?

311
00:12:23,940 --> 00:12:24,440
R.

312
00:12:24,440 --> 00:12:27,180
R, so you can erase that one.

313
00:12:27,180 --> 00:12:28,700
And now you're player one.

314
00:12:28,700 --> 00:12:31,620
And you see, wow, I can either go and do up and get 0,

315
00:12:31,620 --> 00:12:33,620
or I can do down and get 15.

316
00:12:33,620 --> 00:12:36,860
Yes, so yes, then, just erasing that whole thing.

317
00:12:36,860 --> 00:12:40,540
And then we have solved this game through backwards

318
00:12:40,540 --> 00:12:41,900
deduction.

319
00:12:41,900 --> 00:12:46,580
All right, so it is a finite game of information.

320
00:12:46,580 --> 00:12:50,860
So yes, so actually implicit here in showing that,

321
00:12:50,860 --> 00:12:53,160
in showing multiple equilibrium, is precisely

322
00:12:53,160 --> 00:12:54,980
that it's simultaneous.

323
00:12:54,980 --> 00:12:59,780
So again, as I said, so you can either say simultaneous,

324
00:12:59,780 --> 00:13:01,340
or you can say sequential, but you

325
00:13:01,340 --> 00:13:03,300
don't get to find out what the other person did

326
00:13:03,300 --> 00:13:08,300
until you have decided upon what you're doing.

327
00:13:08,300 --> 00:13:10,940
That's a very good question.

328
00:13:10,940 --> 00:13:14,180
And let's see, then we could go and take

329
00:13:14,180 --> 00:13:16,340
a look at one more game.

330
00:13:16,340 --> 00:13:18,460
So let's take a look here.

331
00:13:18,460 --> 00:13:21,660
It's actually, do I still have it up on the board?

332
00:13:21,660 --> 00:13:22,580
Oh, yeah, I do.

333
00:13:22,580 --> 00:13:24,380
I don't have to draw it again.

334
00:13:24,380 --> 00:13:28,780
So let's see, is it exactly the same?

335
00:13:28,780 --> 00:13:30,020
Is it slightly switched?

336
00:13:30,020 --> 00:13:33,500
Let me just quickly fix it.

337
00:13:33,500 --> 00:13:34,980
I'm going to do it.

338
00:13:44,420 --> 00:13:45,220
OK, zero.

339
00:13:45,220 --> 00:13:47,140
All right, so remember that game with Popeye,

340
00:13:47,140 --> 00:13:49,740
where Popeye wants to be on the subway with the other guy.

341
00:13:49,740 --> 00:13:51,940
The other guy wants to be on, but doesn't want to.

342
00:13:51,940 --> 00:13:52,980
Popeye just wants to be with him.

343
00:13:52,980 --> 00:13:54,480
It doesn't matter if he's on or off.

344
00:13:54,480 --> 00:13:56,700
And the other guy does not want to be with Popeye.

345
00:13:56,700 --> 00:13:59,860
All right, so if we go through and try to apply this,

346
00:13:59,860 --> 00:14:02,340
so can on be an astral equilibrium?

347
00:14:02,340 --> 00:14:04,340
No, because who wants to do something different

348
00:14:04,340 --> 00:14:06,740
if it's on on?

349
00:14:06,740 --> 00:14:08,580
The criminal wants to get off.

350
00:14:08,580 --> 00:14:09,660
So that's not one.

351
00:14:09,660 --> 00:14:11,940
How about if it is off on?

352
00:14:11,940 --> 00:14:14,500
Then is that a possible equilibrium?

353
00:14:14,500 --> 00:14:17,220
No, because then, so if the criminal is off,

354
00:14:17,220 --> 00:14:19,300
then what does Popeye want to do?

355
00:14:19,300 --> 00:14:21,260
He wants to get off too, so then you track him.

356
00:14:21,260 --> 00:14:22,620
So that one's not going to work.

357
00:14:22,620 --> 00:14:24,460
What if the criminal's on and Popeye's off?

358
00:14:24,460 --> 00:14:25,220
No, same reason.

359
00:14:25,220 --> 00:14:27,380
Then Popeye would want to get on too.

360
00:14:27,380 --> 00:14:29,460
What if they're both off?

361
00:14:29,460 --> 00:14:30,540
Is that going to work?

362
00:14:30,540 --> 00:14:32,500
No, because then the criminal wants to get on.

363
00:14:32,500 --> 00:14:36,060
And that's another case where we have no Nash equilibrium,

364
00:14:36,060 --> 00:14:37,180
which is also possible.

365
00:14:40,060 --> 00:14:42,020
So pure structure Nash equilibrium concept

366
00:14:42,020 --> 00:14:45,020
is the most used in all of economics and game theory,

367
00:14:45,020 --> 00:14:47,860
and probably all social science, actually.

368
00:14:47,860 --> 00:14:51,220
So it is used all the time, tons of papers.

369
00:14:51,220 --> 00:14:53,660
It's totally possible you guys will one day have a paper

370
00:14:53,660 --> 00:14:56,940
where you will use Nash equilibrium.

371
00:14:56,940 --> 00:14:59,460
It's a little bit odd, because it

372
00:14:59,460 --> 00:15:04,180
seems to assume away strategic interaction, which in a way

373
00:15:04,180 --> 00:15:07,300
was what game theory was adopted to address.

374
00:15:07,300 --> 00:15:09,060
Because notice, with Nash equilibrium,

375
00:15:09,060 --> 00:15:11,100
no one is ever thinking ahead and saying, well,

376
00:15:11,100 --> 00:15:12,700
if I change, then he'll do something different,

377
00:15:12,700 --> 00:15:14,980
which might make me even worse off than it was before,

378
00:15:14,980 --> 00:15:17,340
so I'm just going to sit here.

379
00:15:17,340 --> 00:15:21,140
Like, for example, you might say,

380
00:15:21,140 --> 00:15:22,500
I'm not going to change my seat.

381
00:15:22,500 --> 00:15:24,780
I would like that other seat, but if I change that seat,

382
00:15:24,780 --> 00:15:26,420
some other people will change their seat,

383
00:15:26,420 --> 00:15:27,660
and there will be a chain reaction.

384
00:15:27,660 --> 00:15:29,080
And in the end, I'm actually going

385
00:15:29,080 --> 00:15:31,420
to like the equilibrium less, because my friends won't

386
00:15:31,420 --> 00:15:33,020
be sitting next to me anymore.

387
00:15:33,020 --> 00:15:36,460
And so I'm actually better off not doing what appears,

388
00:15:36,460 --> 00:15:39,180
what I actually would make me better off holding constant

389
00:15:39,180 --> 00:15:42,860
with other people do, because what other people do

390
00:15:42,860 --> 00:15:45,660
won't be constant if I change my behavior.

391
00:15:45,660 --> 00:15:47,140
OK.

392
00:15:47,140 --> 00:15:49,100
All right, so anyway, so a more strategic player

393
00:15:49,100 --> 00:15:50,380
might think I'm not going to switch just

394
00:15:50,380 --> 00:15:52,100
because I'd be better off holding my opponent's action

395
00:15:52,100 --> 00:15:53,180
constant.

396
00:15:53,180 --> 00:15:55,180
Maybe it'll respond in a way that makes me wish

397
00:15:55,180 --> 00:15:57,020
I hadn't changed in the first place.

398
00:15:57,020 --> 00:15:59,140
The standard game theorist answer to that

399
00:15:59,140 --> 00:16:01,260
is we can just go and expand the game

400
00:16:01,260 --> 00:16:02,820
and add more structure to the game

401
00:16:02,820 --> 00:16:04,620
in order to take it out of that.

402
00:16:04,620 --> 00:16:08,740
So that's not totally crazy, although it's more often

403
00:16:08,740 --> 00:16:10,460
they just say that without ever doing it.

404
00:16:10,460 --> 00:16:12,980
But in principle, could be, yeah.

405
00:16:12,980 --> 00:16:17,180
All right, so questions about pure strategy Nash equilibrium?

406
00:16:17,180 --> 00:16:17,680
Yes?

407
00:16:17,680 --> 00:16:21,220
Is the Nash equilibrium always prior to efficiency?

408
00:16:21,220 --> 00:16:22,640
Because from the example you gave.

409
00:16:22,640 --> 00:16:24,300
Oh, absolutely not.

410
00:16:24,300 --> 00:16:30,500
So remember that, as I said, every strictly dominant outcome

411
00:16:30,500 --> 00:16:31,900
is Nash equilibrium.

412
00:16:31,900 --> 00:16:33,860
And remember that in the prisoner's dilemma,

413
00:16:33,860 --> 00:16:36,820
that is a textbook case of an outcome that is actually

414
00:16:36,820 --> 00:16:38,900
prior to inefficient.

415
00:16:38,900 --> 00:16:43,340
So in the prisoner's dilemma, both people confess,

416
00:16:43,340 --> 00:16:46,820
and they're both worse off than if neither confessed.

417
00:16:46,820 --> 00:16:47,740
Remember that?

418
00:16:47,740 --> 00:16:50,340
And that's actually a perfectly good Nash equilibrium

419
00:16:50,340 --> 00:16:52,460
for them to both confess.

420
00:16:52,460 --> 00:16:54,460
So yeah, there's no promise at all

421
00:16:54,460 --> 00:16:57,500
that this is going to be prior to efficient.

422
00:16:57,500 --> 00:16:59,260
Other than what I said in week one, which

423
00:16:59,260 --> 00:17:01,900
is everything the real world is because of all those things

424
00:17:01,900 --> 00:17:02,420
I told you.

425
00:17:02,420 --> 00:17:05,780
But again, in the context of a game,

426
00:17:05,780 --> 00:17:07,780
even on a homework problem, there's

427
00:17:07,780 --> 00:17:11,460
no presumption that a Nash equilibrium will be efficient.

428
00:17:11,460 --> 00:17:13,900
In fact, the very fact someone's bothering to give it

429
00:17:13,900 --> 00:17:15,480
to you as a homework problem, probably

430
00:17:15,480 --> 00:17:17,900
you're going to have to prove that it's not efficient.

431
00:17:17,900 --> 00:17:20,220
Otherwise, it's not a smart homework problem.

432
00:17:20,220 --> 00:17:21,820
Because you might give 10 homework problems

433
00:17:21,820 --> 00:17:23,740
and three are efficient and seven aren't.

434
00:17:23,740 --> 00:17:25,940
And then you have to figure out which ones are which.

435
00:17:25,940 --> 00:17:27,420
So that might be a homework problem.

436
00:17:30,100 --> 00:17:32,300
Now, notice that I immediately started

437
00:17:32,300 --> 00:17:35,020
talking about pure strategy Nash equilibrium

438
00:17:35,020 --> 00:17:36,380
when it seems like I'm giving you

439
00:17:36,380 --> 00:17:39,340
two extra letters for no reason.

440
00:17:39,340 --> 00:17:41,460
Why is it pure strategy Nash equilibrium?

441
00:17:41,460 --> 00:17:43,340
Can't you just say Nash equilibrium?

442
00:17:43,340 --> 00:17:47,660
And the answer is that I am predistinguishing

443
00:17:47,660 --> 00:17:50,020
pure strategy Nash equilibrium for the other kind of Nash

444
00:17:50,020 --> 00:17:51,940
equilibrium, which we will now talk about,

445
00:17:51,940 --> 00:17:56,420
which is the MSNE, the mixed strategy Nash equilibrium.

446
00:17:59,660 --> 00:18:02,300
And that is the other thing to think about whenever you're

447
00:18:02,300 --> 00:18:04,140
talking about Nash equilibrium.

448
00:18:04,140 --> 00:18:06,300
After you have done what I've just talked about,

449
00:18:06,300 --> 00:18:09,100
where you go over every one of the cells

450
00:18:09,100 --> 00:18:11,740
and see whether it's a Nash equilibrium,

451
00:18:11,740 --> 00:18:15,580
that will yield a list of all the pure strategy Nash

452
00:18:15,580 --> 00:18:16,860
equilibrium.

453
00:18:16,860 --> 00:18:18,580
But then you are not done.

454
00:18:18,580 --> 00:18:21,420
If you have been instructed to find all Nash equilibrium,

455
00:18:21,420 --> 00:18:25,340
then you must go and search also for the mixed strategy Nash

456
00:18:25,340 --> 00:18:26,700
equilibrium.

457
00:18:26,700 --> 00:18:29,820
Mixed strategy Nash equilibrium.

458
00:18:29,820 --> 00:18:32,940
So instead of asking whether any players incentive

459
00:18:32,940 --> 00:18:35,540
change strategies, you can also ask

460
00:18:35,540 --> 00:18:38,060
whether any players incentive changes probabilities

461
00:18:38,060 --> 00:18:40,780
of playing different strategies.

462
00:18:40,780 --> 00:18:44,260
So instead of saying, do you want to change what you do,

463
00:18:44,260 --> 00:18:47,100
you could say, do you want to change the dice you're rolling

464
00:18:47,100 --> 00:18:48,620
for what you're doing?

465
00:18:48,620 --> 00:18:51,420
Do you want to change the pattern or the probability

466
00:18:51,420 --> 00:18:55,460
of the frequency with which you play different things?

467
00:18:55,460 --> 00:18:58,940
So first, as a technical matter, how

468
00:18:58,940 --> 00:19:02,220
do you solve for mixed strategy Nash equilibrium?

469
00:19:02,220 --> 00:19:05,460
Now, this is one of the ideas that people

470
00:19:05,460 --> 00:19:08,300
find most counterintuitive in all game theory.

471
00:19:08,300 --> 00:19:09,260
But it's simple.

472
00:19:09,260 --> 00:19:11,220
It just doesn't make sense to people at first.

473
00:19:11,220 --> 00:19:14,420
You have to convince yourself by doing it a bunch of times.

474
00:19:14,420 --> 00:19:16,540
Although I'll also try to convince you intuitively

475
00:19:16,540 --> 00:19:18,700
once we see how to solve for it.

476
00:19:18,700 --> 00:19:22,660
See, I feel like I have some modest success.

477
00:19:22,660 --> 00:19:24,540
But it's ultimately the kind of thing

478
00:19:24,540 --> 00:19:26,140
you've got to convince yourself.

479
00:19:26,140 --> 00:19:28,460
So how can this be?

480
00:19:28,460 --> 00:19:30,140
So how do you do it?

481
00:19:30,140 --> 00:19:31,500
Here's the answer.

482
00:19:31,500 --> 00:19:33,780
Each player must play a mixture that leaves

483
00:19:33,780 --> 00:19:37,340
all other players indifferent.

484
00:19:37,340 --> 00:19:39,220
And you're like, what?

485
00:19:39,220 --> 00:19:44,940
Why would I do whatever makes other people indifferent?

486
00:19:44,940 --> 00:19:46,820
Or the way people often think about it is,

487
00:19:46,820 --> 00:19:48,100
why should I wake up in the morning

488
00:19:48,100 --> 00:19:49,980
and say, you know what I'm going to do today?

489
00:19:49,980 --> 00:19:51,820
Whatever makes other people indifferent.

490
00:19:51,820 --> 00:19:54,140
That seems like a very strange goal.

491
00:19:54,140 --> 00:19:56,420
That's not the right way to think about it.

492
00:19:56,420 --> 00:19:58,180
Rather, what we need to say is, look,

493
00:19:58,180 --> 00:20:16,220
what is the only thing that is stable?


